ards, feeling cards, the start-middle-finish card, Curio Class's own. Free path: the teacher hums or claps where no music player is at hand; found objects for starting points. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 1 because changing one element needs the elements card, and a dance needs the stop cue and the safe space; before unit 3 because a change needs a dance to change. The inspiration-list and start-middle-finish models start here | Candidate: a doubled parent-and-child version of the partner feeling dance for the home room | Feelings: feeling words come from a card; no child is asked what makes them sad or angry. Spirit of the Peacock: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's own published words and a Chinese cultural body's page on Dai dance first; children make their own animal dance and do not copy her moves or wear a costume. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; objects for starting points are large and soft, nothing small enough to mouth. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 3 | K | Watch, tell and change. *What do we notice when we watch a dance, and how can a dancer change a move?* | 14 | da-k-revise; da-k-respond | DA:Cr3.1.Ka; DA:Cr3.1.Kb; DA:Re7.1.Ka; DA:Re7.1.Kb; DA:Re8.1.Ka; DA:Re9.1.Ka | Responding (Re7, Re8, Re9); creating (Cr3). Works: Savion Glover, a filmed tap piece the unit plan picks (United States, born 1973, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to a museum's or a dance archive's page, where a rhythm and a step come back again and again; Kim Baek-bong, Buchaechum, the Korean fan dance (Korea, first staged in 1954), described and linked to a Korean cultural body's page, where fans open again and again into one big flower shape | DA:Cr3.1.Ka: in a guided improvisation, change a move when the teacher suggests a new way (bigger, smaller, lower, slower, from the suggestion card). DA:Cr3.1.Kb: draw a picture or a symbol for one move (a spiral for a turn, an arrow for a jump). DA:Re7.1.Ka: notice a move that comes back in a dance. DA:Re7.1.Kb: show or describe moves watched or done. DA:Re8.1.Ka: describe moves watched with easy dance words (fast, slow, high, low, floppy, sharp, from unit 1). DA:Re9.1.Ka: show a move that stood out in a dance and say why. Words: watch, again, change, symbol, stand out, because. Showing: each group's changed dance from unit 2, shown with its symbol card. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: day A (showing), DA:Cr3.1.Ka, a look-for: the child's unit 2 dance danced, then danced again with the change the teacher suggests at the time; DA:Re9.1.Ka, a show item and one talk item: the child shows the move that stood out in the tap or the fan dance and says why; DA:Cr3.1.Kb, a kept card made on the last making day: one move drawn as a picture or symbol, scored after the session; day B (watching, an unseen Curio Class phrase danced by the teacher), DA:Re7.1.Ka, a show item: the child shows the move that came back; DA:Re7.1.Kb, a show item: the child shows one other move from the phrase; DA:Re8.1.Ka, a talk item: one move described with an easy dance word. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a pattern made by repeats (grade 1), moves that stand for an idea (grade 1). Tools: the suggestion card, symbol cards, work cards, the unseen phrase's step card, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil and scrap paper for symbols. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 2 because a change is made to the child's own dance; the dance words come from unit 1. Before unit 4 so the dance shown to families has been changed once and the class has watched as an audience. The try-a-change and look-and-say models start here | Candidate: a third short dance by a living maker from Africa, described and linked, for range in year K | Buchaechum: the sensitivity agent reads a Korean cultural body's page first; no fan dance costume, and children's paper fans carry their own designs. Tap: children do not tap on hard floors in shoes; rhythms are clapped or stamped softly in bare feet. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 4 | K | Our dance for families. *How do we share a dance with others, and what can a dance or a picture make us feel?* | 12 | da-k-stage; da-k-connect | DA:Pr6.1.Ka; DA:Pr6.1.Kb; DA:Cn10.1.Ka; DA:Cn10.1.Kb; DA:Cn11.1.Ka | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: Gene Kelly, the title dance of Singin' in the Rain (United States, 1952 film), a dance with an umbrella as its prop, described and linked, never copied; Henri Matisse, Dance (I) (France, 1909, the Museum of Modern Art, New York), a painting of dancers in a ring, described and linked, the artwork the class studies and moves from | DA:Pr6.1.Ka: dance in the marked dance area for others and alongside them (the class's dance, in groups). DA:Pr6.1.Kb: pick one prop for the dance (a scarf, a paper umbrella, a paper fan from unit 3) and say what it adds. DA:Cn10.1.Ka: name a feeling a dance gives and tie it to the child's life, or to a story character's or an invented child's, marked the same (the like-me line). DA:Cn10.1.Kb: study the Matisse painting, move to show what is interesting in it, and ask a question about it. DA:Cn11.1.Ka: describe or show moves from a dance watched or done (the umbrella dance, or one from units 1 to 3). Words: audience, dance area, prop, feeling, painting, question. Showing: the class's dance for families at the year's end, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: day A (at the teacher's spot), DA:Cn10.1.Ka, a talk item: a feeling the umbrella dance gives and what it is like in the child's life or an invented child's; DA:Cn10.1.Kb, a show item and one talk item: a move for what the child finds interesting in the painting, and one question asked about it; DA:Cn11.1.Ka, a show item: moves from a dance watched or done, shown or described; day B (the family showing), DA:Pr6.1.Ka, a look-for: dances in the marked area for the audience and alongside the group; DA:Pr6.1.Kb, a look-for: the picked prop used in the dance. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: an audience seated apart (grade 1), a story danced (grade 1). Tools: the dance-area floor plan, prop cards, the like-me card, the painting's work card, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper umbrellas and fans from scrap paper; any soft cloth for scarves. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in the year because the family dance is built from the year's dances (units 2 and 3) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 26), outside the core checks of weeks 29 to 32; this unit's family showing is its own assessment. The performing-space and like-me-line models start here | Candidate: the doubled solo-with-parent version of the family dance for the home room | Ties to the child's own life are invited, never required: the invented child's line is offered beside it and marked the same; health's steps if a child tells something. A child's dance is shown outside the class only with the family's yes; no photographs without it. Props: soft and short, no scarf tied round a neck, paper umbrellas with no metal parts. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 5 | 1 | Lines, circles and paths. *How do dancers travel together in lines and circles, and how do paths, speeds and qualities change a dance?* | 18 | da-g1-technique; da-g1-elements | DA:Pr5.1.1a; DA:Pr5.1.1b; DA:Pr5.1.1c; DA:Pr4.1.1a; DA:Pr4.1.1b; DA:Pr4.1.1c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding touched on the two works (review). Works: Carmencita, Carmencita (Spain, a dancer filmed at Thomas Edison's studio in the United States, 1894, the Library of Congress, public domain), quick turns and sharp arms; the Ballet FolklA3rico de MAcxico, founded by Amalia HernA�ndez in 1952 (Mexico; a company that dances today), a folklA3rico dance the unit plan picks, danced in lines and circles with zigzag paths, described and linked to the company's or a museum's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: kindergarten's start and stop cue and own space (da-k-technique) and the elements card (da-k-elements). DA:Pr5.1.1a: show many ways to travel and to stay still, with different shapes, patterns and directions. DA:Pr5.1.1b: move safely in group activities and formations (a line, a circle, scattered), keeping one's own space. DA:Pr5.1.1c: change moves and the group's layout when asked (line to circle on the call). DA:Pr4.1.1a: change shape, level and facing; travel curved and zigzag paths; form lines and circles. DA:Pr4.1.1b: move at quick, medium and slow speeds, and keep a steady beat as the drum's tempo changes. DA:Pr4.1.1c: show movement qualities and name them with describing words (bouncy, floppy, smooth, sharp). Words: path, zigzag, curved, facing, steady beat, bouncy. Showing: a paths-and-formations dance for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: day A, DA:Pr5.1.1a, a look-for: three ways to travel and two to stay still, each with a different shape or direction; DA:Pr5.1.1b, a look-for: own space kept in a line and in a circle; DA:Pr5.1.1c, a look-for: the move and the layout changed on the call; day B, DA:Pr4.1.1a, a look-for: a curved and a zigzag path traveled, with a change of level and of facing, ending in a line or a circle; DA:Pr4.1.1b, a look-for: a steady beat kept at quick, medium and slow drum tempos; DA:Pr4.1.1c, a show item and one talk item: a quality danced and named in a describing word. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: symmetrical shapes and strong beats (grade 2). Tools: the formation cards, the path cards (curved, zigzag), the elements card, work cards, Curio Class's own; a beat keeper or the drum. Free path: chalk or tape paths; claps for the beat. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 1 because it reopens the dance space and the cue after the summer and moves from one's own spot to formations; both kindergarten models (dance-warm-up, dance-elements) are retrieved where first needed. The quality words named here are what unit 7's response uses | Candidate: a dance in lines and circles from Africa or Asia beside the folklA3rico, described and linked | FolklA3rico: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own published words and a Mexican cultural body's page first; no costume, no skirt work imitated, children make their own formation dance. Connection: physical education K to 2 unit 13 (g1-dance-moves) teaches dance moves as motor skill in grade 1 weeks 23 to 27, after this unit; this unit uses kindergarten's travels as material and teaches no motor cue. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; traveling in a line keeps an arm's length, no pulling or holding hands unless both agree. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 6 | 1 | Where dances begin. *What can spark a dance, and how do dancers pick moves that show a feeling or follow the music?* | 14 | da-g1-ideas; da-g1-form | DA:Cr1.1.1a; DA:Cr1.1.1b; DA:Cr2.1.1a; DA:Cr2.1.1b | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: Alvin Ailey, Revelations (United States, 1960; Ailey died 1989), a dance sparked by the songs he remembered from his childhood in Texas, described and linked to the company's page; Akram Khan, Chotto Desh (United Kingdom, 2015, a family version of his dance about his childhood and his family's Bangladeshi heritage; born 1974, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: kindergarten's starting points (da-k-ideas) and start, middle and finish (da-k-form). DA:Cr1.1.1a: try moves sparked by a starting point (a poem, a picture, a sound) and name what inspired them. DA:Cr1.1.1b: try traveling and in-place moves by changing an element (speed, level or size) on purpose. DA:Cr2.1.1a: improvise a sequence with a start, middle and finish, and say why one choice was made. DA:Cr2.1.1b: pick moves that show a feeling or idea, or that follow a bit of music. Words: inspire, starting point, element, sequence, choice, feeling. Showing: the class's starting-point dances for a partner class, each with its card. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: day A, DA:Cr1.1.1a, a look-for and one talk item: moves from the drawn starting-point card, and the child names what sparked them; DA:Cr1.1.1b, a look-for: a travel and an in-place move, each changed in one element on purpose; day B, DA:Cr2.1.1a, a look-for and one talk item: a sequence with a start, middle and finish, and why one choice was made; DA:Cr2.1.1b, a look-for: moves that show the feeling on the child's card or follow the music played. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: linking several moves into a phrase (grade 2), a central idea (grade 2). Tools: starting-point cards, feeling cards, the start-middle-finish card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a poem read aloud, a picture card, claps. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 5 because changing an element on purpose needs unit 5's paths, levels and speeds; before unit 7 because a remembered sequence to change needs a sequence made here | Candidate: a second starting-point dance with no religious source, for a school that leaves out Revelations | Revelations is set to spirituals, religious songs of Black Americans: the unit names them as songs from Ailey's childhood church and studies how songs sparked moves, with no child asked about faith; the sensitivity agent reads the company's own published words and a museum's page on spirituals first (leave-out line). Chotto Desh: the sensitivity agent reads the artist's published words first; no child is asked about their own family's country. Feeling cards as unit 2. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 7 | 1 | Patterns and meanings. *How do moves make patterns and stand for ideas, and how can a dancer change a remembered dance?* | 14 | da-g1-revise; da-g1-respond | DA:Cr3.1.1a; DA:Cr3.1.1b; DA:Re7.1.1a; DA:Re7.1.1b; DA:Re8.1.1a; DA:Re9.1.1a | Responding (Re7, Re8, Re9); creating (Cr3). Works: Kpanlogo, a social dance of the Ga people of Accra, Ghana, begun in the early 1960s to new drum music, danced today, described and linked to a Ghanaian cultural body's or a museum's page; a Bharatanatyam piece the unit plan picks (India, classical dance), a non-devotional piece whose hand gestures stand for a bird, a flower or a deer, described and linked to a named living teacher's or dancer's own published teaching | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: kindergarten's suggested change and symbol (da-k-revise) and watch, show and say (da-k-respond). DA:Cr3.1.1a: try suggested changes to moves while improvising and when repeating a short remembered sequence. DA:Cr3.1.1b: draw pictures or symbols for kinds of moves: jump, turn, slide. DA:Re7.1.1a: notice a repeating move that forms a pattern in a dance. DA:Re7.1.1b: show and describe moves watched from a particular style or culture (Kpanlogo or Bharatanatyam). DA:Re8.1.1a: pick moves that stand for an idea and say how, in simple terms (the hand gestures). DA:Re9.1.1a: show several eye-catching moves and say what makes them interesting and why they were chosen. Words: pattern, repeat, style, culture, stand for, interesting. Showing: each group's remembered sequence, changed, with its symbol card. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: day A (showing), DA:Cr3.1.1a, a look-for: a change tried in an improvisation and a change tried in the remembered sequence; DA:Re9.1.1a, a show item and one talk item: two eye-catching moves from the two dances shown, and what makes them interesting; DA:Cr3.1.1b, a kept card made on the last making day: symbols for jump, turn and slide, scored after the session; day B (watching, an unseen Curio Class phrase danced by the teacher), DA:Re7.1.1a, a show item: the repeating move that forms the pattern; DA:Re7.1.1b, a show item and one talk item: a Kpanlogo or Bharatanatyam move shown and described; DA:Re8.1.1a, a talk item: a move in the unseen phrase that stands for an idea, and how. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: patterns that build, two styles compared (grade 2). Tools: symbol cards, the suggestion card, work cards, the unseen phrase's step card, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps for the Kpanlogo rhythm; pencil and scrap paper. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 6 because the remembered sequence to change is unit 6's; quality words from unit 5 carry the descriptions. Before unit 8 because another culture's movement types and a story's ideas build on moves that stand for ideas. The dance-look-describe-interpret model starts here | Candidate: the Bharatanatyam piece confirmed non-devotional by its teacher's own published words | Kpanlogo: the sensitivity agent reads a Ghanaian cultural body's page or a museum's page and a named Ga teacher's published words first; children clap the rhythm and dance their own pattern, no costume. Bharatanatyam grew in part from temple dance: the unit picks a non-devotional piece, studies the gestures by watching, and children invent their own gestures for an idea; no devotional piece or gesture is copied (leave-out line). Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 8 | 1 | Dancing a story for an audience. *How do dancers tell a story for an audience, and how do dances from other places use movement?* | 14 | da-g1-stage; da-g1-connect | DA:Pr6.1.1a; DA:Pr6.1.1b; DA:Cn10.1.1a; DA:Cn10.1.1b; DA:Cn11.1.1a | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: a Chinese silk ribbon dance the unit plan picks (China, a folk form danced in schools and dance schools today), long ribbons moved in circles and waves, described and linked to a Chinese cultural body's or a museum's page; Beatrix Potter, The Tale of Peter Rabbit (United Kingdom, 1902, its pictures public domain in the United States), the picture-book story the class dances | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: kindergarten's marked dance area (da-k-stage) and the like-me line (da-k-connect). DA:Pr6.1.1a: dance for an audience seated apart from the dancers. DA:Pr6.1.1b: try simple props (a short ribbon on a paper tube, a scarf) and judge whether each improves the dance. DA:Cn10.1.1a: find a familiar experience in a dance (running from someone, hiding, a garden) and name the moves that show it, from the child's life or an invented child's, marked the same. DA:Cn10.1.1b: study the story's pictures, talk about them, and dance the story's big ideas (sneaking in, being chased, getting home safe). DA:Cn11.1.1a: watch a dance from another culture (the ribbon dance) and describe its movement types (traveling, turning, waving). Words: audience, seated, prop, story, big idea, culture. Showing: the class's story dance for families at the year's end, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: day A (at the teacher's spot), DA:Cn10.1.1a, a talk item: a familiar experience in the story dance or the ribbon dance and the moves that show it; DA:Cn11.1.1a, a talk item: the ribbon dance's movement types, with one shown; day B (the family showing), DA:Pr6.1.1a, a look-for: dances for the audience seated apart, facing them; DA:Pr6.1.1b, a look-for and one spoken line: the prop tried in the dance and whether it made the dance better; DA:Cn10.1.1b, a look-for: the story's big ideas shown in the group's dance. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: separate zones and staging extras (grade 2), a dance's origin (grade 2). Tools: the audience floor plan, prop cards, the story's picture cards, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: crepe or scrap paper ribbons on a cardboard tube; any soft cloth. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | Last in grade 1 because the story dance is built from the year's making and changing (units 6 and 7) and ends the year after the year-end check (week 25); the dance-two-ties model starts here, where a second culture's dance is first needed | Candidate: the ribbon dance's source confirmed as a Chinese cultural body's own teaching | Ribbon dance: the sensitivity agent reads a Chinese cultural body's page first; no costume, and children's ribbons carry their own movement. Being chased in the story is played lightly; no child plays a hunter catching a classmate, and no chasing between children (each group dances the chase in place). Ties to life invited, never required, with the invented option. Props: ribbons short (no longer than the child's arm span) on soft paper tubes, never tied round a body; space doubled for ribbon days. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 9 | 2 | Pathways and strong beats. *How do dancers keep their own space while traveling pathways together, and how do shapes and beats give a dance its order?* | 18 | da-g2-technique; da-g2-elements | DA:Pr5.1.2a; DA:Pr5.1.2b; DA:Pr5.1.2c; DA:Pr4.1.2a; DA:Pr4.1.2b; DA:Pr4.1.2c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding touched on the two works (review). Works: Lev Ivanov, the Dance of the Little Swans from Swan Lake (Russia, 1895, to Pyotr Tchaikovsky's music of 1876, public domain), four dancers in a line making the same symmetrical moves on the strong beat, described and linked to a museum's or a ballet company's page; tinikling (the Philippines, a folk dance danced today, in three-beat music), dancers stepping between clapping bamboo poles, described and linked to a Philippine cultural body's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: formations and changes on a call (da-g1-technique) and paths, speeds and quality words (da-g1-elements). DA:Pr5.1.2a: perform traveling and in-place moves, body patterns and sequences that travel by different pathways. DA:Pr5.1.2b: move safely in different group formations and spatial relationships (near, far, around, through), sharing and keeping personal space. DA:Pr5.1.2c: repeat moves while aware of others, and adjust moves or position on request; the row's description names only the repeat, and the unit teaches both (For the lead). DA:Pr4.1.2a: move with clear direction; spot symmetrical and uneven shapes; tell circling (traveling round a point) from turning (spinning in place); the row's description leaves out circling and turning, and the unit teaches it (For the lead). DA:Pr4.1.2b: tell short from long phrases, and land on the strong beat in two- and three-beat music (counted 1 2, 1 2 3). DA:Pr4.1.2c: choose fitting describing words for qualities and dance them with body awareness. Words: pathway, symmetrical, circling, turning, phrase, strong beat. Showing: a pathways dance in formations for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two check days), id by id: day A, DA:Pr5.1.2a, a look-for: a short sequence traveling a set pathway with an in-place pattern in the middle; DA:Pr5.1.2b, a look-for: own space kept in two formations and one spatial relationship (around or through); DA:Pr5.1.2c, a look-for: the sequence repeated aware of others, then a position adjusted on the teacher's request; day B, DA:Pr4.1.2a, a look-for and a show item: a clear direction traveled, a symmetrical and an uneven shape shown and pointed out on two shape cards, a circle and a turn danced and told apart; DA:Pr4.1.2b, a look-for and one talk item: landings on the strong beat in a two-beat and a three-beat piece, and a short and a long clapped phrase told apart; DA:Pr4.1.2c, a look-for and one talk item: a quality word chosen for a move and danced with care for the body. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: distance and three dimensions (grade 3). Tools: pathway cards, shape cards, the elements card, work cards, Curio Class's own; a beat keeper or the drum. Free path: tape lines for the tinikling poles; claps for two- and three-beat counts. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 2 because it reopens the dance space after the summer and moves from formations to pathways and spatial relationships; strong beats and phrases come here because unit 10's linked phrase needs them | Candidate: a dance by a living maker from Africa with clear pathways, described and linked | Tinikling: watched only with poles; children step between tape lines on the floor, never between moving poles. The sensitivity agent reads a Philippine cultural body's page first; no costume. Swan Lake: no tutus or pointe work; children make their own symmetrical line dance. Counting beats is counting to three only (no math beyond grade 2). Connection: physical education K to 2 unit 21 (g2-dance-sequence) teaches a linked move sequence as motor skill in grade 2 weeks 24 to 26, after this unit; this unit teaches no motor cue. Hazards and controls