notes         : Dance in a marked area for others and alongside them, and choose one prop to use in a dance. What 
                changes: first year, a marked space and one prop. Assessed by a short showing marked met, partly or 
                not yet on a three-line guide. Teacher note: a child may dance in a pair or watch first; no stage 
                lights or hot materials.

strand        : da
grade         : 1
skill_id      : da-g1-stage
standard      : DA:Pr6.1.1a; DA:Pr6.1.1b
assumes       : da-k-stage
hands_on      : da-g2-stage
model_in      : performing-space
model_out     : performing-space
words         : audience; seated; apart; prop; improve; show
cross_subject : 
notes         : Dance for an audience seated apart from the dancers, and try simple props to see if they improve a 
                performance. What changes: the audience is seated apart, where kindergarten danced beside others in a 
                marked area. Assessed by a short showing to a seated class, scored on a three-line guide.

strand        : da
grade         : 2
skill_id      : da-g2-stage
standard      : DA:Pr6.1.2a; DA:Pr6.1.2b
assumes       : da-g1-stage
hands_on      : da-g3-stage
model_in      : performing-space
model_out     : performing-space
words         : zone; viewer; backdrop; projected; extra; staging
cross_subject : 
notes         : Perform with the audience and dancers in separate marked zones, and add one extra such as a small 
                prop, a backdrop or a projected image. What changes: separate zones and staging extras are added, 
                where grade 1 used simple props. Assessed by a short showing in a marked zone, scored on a three-line 
                guide.

strand        : da
grade         : 3
skill_id      : da-g3-stage
standard      : DA:Pr6.1.3a; DA:Pr6.1.3b
assumes       : da-g2-stage
hands_on      : da-g4-stage
model_in      : performing-space
model_out     : performing-space; stage-terms
words         : left; right; center; costume; scenery; lighting
cross_subject : 
notes         : Learn the stage areas by name (stage left, stage right, center), and add simple costume, music, 
                scenery or light to a dance made for a set space. What changes: stage words and a first design choice, 
                where grade 2 used extras without naming areas. Assessed by a performed dance and a labeled stage 
                sketch, scored on a short rubric.

strand        : da
grade         : K
skill_id      : da-k-respond
standard      : DA:Re7.1.Ka; DA:Re7.1.Kb; DA:Re8.1.Ka; DA:Re9.1.Ka
assumes       : 
hands_on      : da-g1-respond
model_in      : 
model_out     : look-and-say
words         : notice; repeat; describe; stand out; why; dance words
cross_subject : 
notes         : Spot a move that comes back in a dance, show or tell about moves you watched or did, describe 
                movements with easy dance words, and show one movement that stood out and say why. What changes: first 
                year, one repeated move and one favorite. Assessed by a spoken or shown answer marked met, partly or 
                not yet. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be 
                taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against 
                a source before a unit names it.

strand        : da
grade         : 1
skill_id      : da-g1-respond
standard      : DA:Re7.1.1a; DA:Re7.1.1b; DA:Re8.1.1a; DA:Re9.1.1a
assumes       : da-k-respond
hands_on      : da-g2-respond
model_in      : look-and-say
model_out     : look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret
words         : pattern; style; culture; idea; stand for; interesting
cross_subject : 
notes         : Watch a short dance and find a move that repeats to form a pattern, show and describe moves from a 
                style or culture, pick moves that stand for an idea, and say why some moves catch the eye. What 
                changes: patterns and ideas are tied to movement, where kindergarten noticed one repeated move. 
                Assessed by a short showing and a sentence that uses a dance word and points to a move, scored on a 
                three-line guide. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is 
                open to be taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is 
                checked against a source before a unit names it.

strand        : da
grade         : 2
skill_id      : da-g2-respond
standard      : DA:Re7.1.2a; DA:Re7.1.2b; DA:Re8.1.2a; DA:Re9.1.2a
assumes       : da-g1-respond
hands_on      : da-g3-respond
model_in      : look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret
model_out     : look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret
words         : build; pattern; clue; meaning; tradition; works well
cross_subject : 
notes         : Watch dances and find moves that build into a pattern, show and describe moves from different styles 
                or cultures, use movement clues to say what a dance means, and say what makes it work well. What 
                changes: the student points to a clue in the dance to claim a meaning, where grade 1 tied ideas to 
                moves. Assessed by a written or spoken sentence with a clue from the dance, scored on a three-line 
                guide. Teacher note: A sacred or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be 
                taught, and never as an exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against 
                a source before a unit names it.

strand        : da
grade         : 3
skill_id      : da-g3-respond
standard      : DA:Re7.1.3a; DA:Re7.1.3b; DA:Re8.1.3a; DA:Re9.1.3a
assumes       : da-g2-respond
hands_on      : da-g4-respond
model_in      : look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret
model_out     : look-and-say; dance-look-describe-interpret
words         : phrase; pattern; differ; context clue; central idea; similar
cross_subject : ela:SL.3.1 (class talk about a dance that was watched)
notes         : Watch dances and show how a pattern builds a phrase, show how two styles or traditions differ, link 
                movement clues to the dance's main idea, and describe what is alike and different between the styles. 
                What changes: two styles are compared side by side, where grade 2 used clues from one dance. Assessed 
                by a short written response with a reason and a clue, scored on a short rubric. Teacher note: A sacred 
                or ceremonial work is used only when its source says it is open to be taught, and never as an 
                exercise, warm-up, costume or imitation; any cultural claim is checked against a source before a unit 
                names it.

