| # | Year | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Processes, anchors and works | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 3 | Space, time and energy. *How do dancers use space, time and energy, and how do partners keep a dance safe and together?* | 16 | da-g3-technique; da-g3-elements | DA:Pr5.1.3a; DA:Pr5.1.3b; DA:Pr5.1.3c; DA:Pr4.1.3a; DA:Pr4.1.3b; DA:Pr4.1.3c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding touched by watching two dances (look-and-say as review, no claim); creating touched by the class's improvisations. Works: a solo by Germaine Acogny the unit plan picks (born 1944 in Benin, works in Senegal, where she founded the A%cole des Sables at Toubab Dialaw in 1998; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), moves that start from a wave of the spine with the body aligned and strong, described and linked to the school's own page; a Pilobolus piece the unit plan picks (United States, founded 1971, a company that dances today), dancers making shapes in all three dimensions and shaping the empty space between them, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 2's pathways and adjusting (da-g2-technique) and direction, phrases and strong beats (da-g2-elements), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Pr5.1.3a: copy a sequence of shapes, qualities and patterns with the body aligned and strong (the alignment check). DA:Pr5.1.3b: with a partner or a group, change level, direction and path safely (the partner signal: look, nod, go). DA:Pr5.1.3c: recall a sequence in pairs or groups, using a coach's one comment and the student's own self-check to improve it (the recall card). DA:Pr4.1.3a: judge the distance between dancers, move in all three dimensions (up and down, side to side, forward and back), shape filled and empty space, and dance with focus (eyes lead the move). DA:Pr4.1.3b: improvise to fill a set count (8 or 16) while staying with the music's timing and its beat phrasing. DA:Pr4.1.3c: vary energy and dynamics (strong, light, sharp, smooth) to give moves a clear quality for the dance's intent. Words: align, dimension, focus, phrasing, dynamics, energy. Showing: each group's copied sequence and improvisation for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (two showing days), id by id: day 1, DA:Pr5.1.3a, a look-for: the copied sequence danced with the body aligned and strong; DA:Pr5.1.3b, a look-for: a level, direction and path change made with a partner, signaled and spaced; DA:Pr5.1.3c, a look-for and a kept card: the group recalls the sequence with no teacher cue, and the recall card shows the coach's comment, the self-check and the change tried; day 2, DA:Pr4.1.3a, a look-for: distance kept, all three dimensions used, filled and empty space shaped, eyes leading; DA:Pr4.1.3b, a look-for: the improvisation fills the set count and ends on its last beat with the music; DA:Pr4.1.3c, a look-for: two clearly different energy qualities. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: forms (unit 3), eye contact and drum parts (grade 4). Tools: the alignment card, partner signal card, recall card and elements card, Curio Class's own; a beat keeper on the teacher device or a hand drum. Free path: floor tape or chalk spots, a pot and spoon for the beat. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 3 because every later unit dances in shared space with the warm-up, the stop cue and partner safety, and needs the space, time and energy words; it reopens the dance space after the summer. Six ids, all performed and marked live, so it takes two showing days. The phrase-recall model starts here | Candidate: a seated version of each look-for for a student who cannot move freely | Hazards and controls: floor cleared and checked before each session; bare feet or soft-soled shoes, never socks on a smooth floor; an arm's length between dancers when traveling; level changes to the floor slow, knees and hands first, no dropping; no lifts and no weight-sharing (Pilobolus's lifts are watched only, and the class's shapes are made on each student's own spot); partner contact only by agreement and never required; the stop cue every session. Connection: physical education grades 3 to 5 unit 8 (g3-dance-steps) teaches dance steps as motor skill in grade 3 weeks 30 to 32; this unit teaches no motor cue. Acogny: the sensitivity agent reads the A%cole des Sables' own pages and the artist's published words first; students make their own aligned shapes and do not copy her technique as a style. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 2 | 3 | Where dances come from. *Where do dancers find ideas, and what can a dance tell us about people, their relationships and what they care about?* | 16 | da-g3-ideas; da-g3-connect | DA:Cr1.1.3a; DA:Cr1.1.3b; DA:Cn10.1.3a; DA:Cn10.1.3b; DA:Cn11.1.3a | Creating (Cr1); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Katherine Dunham, L'Ag'Ya (United States, 1938; Dunham, 1909 to 2006), a stage dance she made after her 1935 and 1936 research in the Caribbean, from a fighting dance of Martinique, described and linked to the Library of Congress's or a museum's page; Paul Taylor, Esplanade (United States, 1975; Taylor died 2018), dancers walking, running and sliding together as friends and a group, built from everyday moves, described and linked to the company's page; a Dancing Earth piece about caring for water or seeds that the unit plan picks from the company's own pages (Dancing Earth Indigenous Contemporary Dance Creations, founded by Rulan Tangen in 2004, dancers from many Native nations, a company that dances today), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 2's idea sources and linked moves (da-g2-ideas) and a dance's people and place (da-g2-connect), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Cr1.1.3a: pick a starting point of the student's own (a picture, a poem, a sound, an object, a place) and try moves from it, on the student's starting-point list. DA:Cr1.1.3b: take on a movement challenge the teacher sets (cross the floor so your feet never move at the same speed twice; make three shapes, each on different body parts) and choose and show one solution with a one-line reason. DA:Cn10.1.3a: compare the relationships shown in a dance (Esplanade's friends and group) with the student's own relationships, or an invented child's or a story character's, marked the same. DA:Cn10.1.3b: ask a research question about a dance that takes a view on an issue (the Dancing Earth piece and caring for water or seeds), find the answer on Curio Class's research cards, written from the company's own pages and a public agency's page on the issue, explore the key idea in movement, and share what the moves helped them remember or notice. DA:Cn11.1.3a: link a dance's moves to the culture they come from and say what they reveal (L'Ag'Ya's moves and the Martinique fighting dance Dunham studied). Words: starting point, challenge, solution, relationship, research, culture. Showing: the challenge solutions and the research moves shown to the class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr1.1.3a, a look-for: moves made from the student's own starting point, named on the kept starting-point card; DA:Cr1.1.3b, a look-for: the chosen solution to a new challenge card shown, with its one-line reason on the card; DA:Cn10.1.3b, a look-for and a kept card: the research moves shown, and the research card with the question, the answer found and one line on what the moves helped them notice; DA:Cn10.1.3a, a written item: the relationship in an unseen described dance compared with one of the student's own or the invented child's (marked the same); DA:Cn11.1.3a, a written item: two moves of an unseen described dance linked to the culture on its work card, and what they show. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a dance on a school topic from several sources (grade 4), inventing a challenge (grade 4). Tools: the starting-point list, challenge cards, research cards, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: pictures from old magazines, found objects, poems read aloud. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 1 because making from a starting point uses the space, time and energy words and partner safety; before unit 3 because a phrase that shows a feeling or idea needs an idea of the student's own, and comparing two traditions needs this unit's habit of linking moves to their culture. The movement-challenge model starts here | Candidate: a research card set that keeps the issue a question a grade 3 student can answer from two short cards | Ties to the student's own life are invited, never required: the invented child's line is offered beside it and marked the same; health's steps if a student tells something. Dancing Earth: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own published words and a Native-run source first; dancers are named by their own nations as the company names them; the class studies the piece's view on water or seeds by watching, and no student imitates it or wears regalia; no ceremony is studied. Dunham also studied ceremonies in Haiti; the unit uses L'Ag'Ya only, a stage work, and no ceremony; the fighting dance's moves are watched, and students' own moves never strike or touch. The issue stays a school-safe question (caring for water or seeds), and no student is asked to take a side in a public dispute. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; challenge cards ask for nothing higher than a small jump on the floor. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 3 | 3 | Patterns, forms and styles. *How do repeated and reversed moves give a dance its form, and how do two traditions use pattern differently?* | 16 | da-g3-form; da-g3-respond | DA:Cr2.1.3a; DA:Cr2.1.3b; DA:Re7.1.3a; DA:Re7.1.3b; DA:Re8.1.3a; DA:Re9.1.3a | Creating (Cr2); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9); connecting practiced through each tradition's setting (review). Works: Trisha Brown, Accumulation (United States, 1971; Brown died 2017), one move added at a time and the whole repeated, described and linked to the company's page; a kathak solo by a named living dancer the unit plan picks (northern India, a classical dance grown from storytellers and later the royal courts), a non-devotional piece of footwork patterns and spins, described and linked to the dancer's or a cultural body's page; an Irish step dance piece the unit plan picks (Ireland, danced today; the stage show Riverdance first danced in Dublin in 1994 carried it worldwide), described and linked: two traditions of footwork patterns, one with telling arms and spins, one with still arms | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 2's central idea in the middle (da-g2-form) and clues to meaning across styles (da-g2-respond), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Cr2.1.3a: try choreographic tools (repetition, reversal, accumulation) to make simple patterns and AB and ABA forms. DA:Cr2.1.3b: make a phrase that shows a feeling or idea, then discuss how the choices helped show it. DA:Re7.1.3a: spot how a pattern of moves builds one phrase (Accumulation). DA:Re7.1.3b: show and explain how two traditions differ (kathak and Irish step dance: the arms, the upper body, the rhythm of the feet), the showing done in the class's own phrase danced two ways, from step cards written from a named practitioner's published teaching. DA:Re8.1.3a: pick movement clues and explain how they tie to the dance's main idea. DA:Re9.1.3a: pick moves from the two traditions and describe what is alike and different. Class talk practices ELA's SL.3.1 (not owned). Words: pattern, tool, AB, ABA, phrase, style. Showing: each group's ABA dance for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr2.1.3a, a look-for: an AB or ABA form danced, with a tool named on the kept form card; DA:Cr2.1.3b, a look-for and a kept line: the phrase shows its feeling or idea, and the line says how one choice helped; DA:Re7.1.3a, a written item: the pattern that builds the teacher's unseen phrase, in move order; DA:Re8.1.3a, a written item: a clue in the unseen phrase and how it ties to its main idea; DA:Re9.1.3a, a written item: one way the two traditions' moves are alike and one way they differ, from two new work cards; DA:Re7.1.3b, a show item: the class phrase shown both ways at the teacher's spot, with one difference told. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: altering a tool for a study around a main idea (grade 4), styles inside one genre (grade 4). Tools: the tools card, the form card, a two-traditions chart, the response card, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps for the rhythm. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 2 because a phrase that shows a feeling or idea starts from a starting point, and comparing two traditions uses unit 2's link between moves and culture; before unit 4 because the dance revised and staged there is the ABA dance made here. The choreographic-tools model starts here | Candidate: a written-item version read aloud a second time for a student who reads below grade 3 | Kathak: its temple and court history in one plain sentence from the named dancer's or a cultural body's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; a non-devotional piece only; no bells or costume, and no hand gesture of devotional meaning is copied (leave-out line). Irish step dance: no hard shoes; stamping soft in bare feet. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; spins slow, three at most in a row, and stop on the cue (dizziness). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 4 | 3 | Our dance on a stage. *How do dancers change a dance after feedback, and how do stage areas and a design choice help an audience see it?* | 12 | da-g3-revise; da-g3-stage | DA:Cr3.1.3a; DA:Cr3.1.3b; DA:Pr6.1.3a; DA:Pr6.1.3b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr6); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: Oskar Schlemmer, Triadic Ballet (Germany, first danced in 1922; Schlemmer died 1943), costumes built as geometric shapes that change how a body can move, described and linked to a museum's page; kabuki dance (Japan, a theater form begun in the early 1600s, on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage since 2008), Fuji Musume (The Wisteria Maiden, first staged about 1826), with painted scenery, live shamisen music and a costume change on stage, described and linked to a Japanese cultural body's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: grade 2's chosen change and level symbols (da-g2-revise) and zones and a staging extra (da-g2-stage), each with its ten-minute reteach. DA:Cr3.1.3a: use a partner's feedback (describe, ask, suggest) to change moves in the unit 3 dance, then describe what changed. DA:Cr3.1.3b: chart the dance's travel directions and paths on a floor map with symbols. DA:Pr6.1.3a: name the areas of a stage with production terms (stage left, stage right, center, upstage, downstage) and use them. DA:Pr6.1.3b: try a simple costume piece, music, scenery or light in the dance for its set space, and keep one. Words: feedback, change, map, stage left, stage right, scenery. Showing: the year's dance for families on a marked stage, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (one showing day and kept cards), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.3a, a kept card and a look-for: the change card (the partner's suggestion, the change, one line describing it), and the change seen in the showing; DA:Cr3.1.3b, a kept map: the floor map's directions and paths match the dance; DA:Pr6.1.3a, a kept sketch and a look-for: the stage sketch labeled