| 5 | 4 | Rhythm, balance and cues. *How do dancers balance, move together on a cue and answer a drum, and how do they keep their bodies strong and safe?* | 16 | da-g4-technique; da-g4-elements | DA:Pr5.1.4a; DA:Pr5.1.4b; DA:Pr5.1.4c; DA:Pr4.1.4a; DA:Pr4.1.4b; DA:Pr4.1.4c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding touched on the two works (review); creating touched by the drum-and-dance pairs. Works: bomba (Puerto Rico, an Afro-Puerto Rican tradition danced today), where a dancer's sharp moves call the lead drum and the drummer answers each one, a piece by a named living group the unit plan picks, described and linked to a Puerto Rican cultural body's or a museum's page; legong (Bali, Indonesia, a dance made for watching, taught to young dancers today; on UNESCO's list since 2015 among Bali's three genres of traditional dance), with held balances, exact fingers and quick moves of the eyes, described and linked to a cultural body's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 1's aligned sequence and recall (da-g3-technique) and space, time and energy (da-g3-elements). DA:Pr5.1.4a: use alignment and balance when copying and recalling movement patterns. DA:Pr5.1.4b: do a short strengthening routine (wall push, knee plank, calf rises) and explain how technique, avoiding injury and food choices connect, through an invented dancer's week on a card. DA:Pr5.1.4c: match phrases with the other dancers on a music cue or a held-card cue, and use feedback to set the group's next goal. DA:Pr4.1.4a: make fixed and changing shapes, land small jumps softly, and connect with a partner through eye contact. DA:Pr4.1.4b: play a simple drum part for others to dance to, move to steady and uneven rhythms, and react when the tempo changes. DA:Pr4.1.4c: analyze the energy changes in a phrase, describe them in vivid words, then refine the phrase. Words: balance, strengthen, cue, rhythm, tempo, energy. Showing: drum-and-dance pairs for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Pr5.1.4a, a look-for: a balance held in the copied and recalled pattern; DA:Pr5.1.4c, a look-for and a kept card: the group starts and changes together on the cue, and the goal card names a goal set from feedback; DA:Pr4.1.4a, a look-for: a fixed and a changing shape, soft landings, eye contact with a partner; DA:Pr4.1.4b, a look-for: each student plays the group's drum part once and dances once through a tempo change; DA:Pr5.1.4b, a written item and a show item: from a new invented dancer's card, how her warm-up, technique and food choices connect to avoiding injury, and two moves of the strengthening routine shown with alignment at the teacher's spot; DA:Pr4.1.4c, a written item: the energy changes in the teacher's unseen phrase in two vivid words, and one refinement for the class phrase. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: tight against flowing (grade 5). Tools: the rhythm card (steady and uneven patterns in claps), the cue card, the energy word bank and the invented dancer card, Curio Class's own; a beat keeper or a hand drum. Free path: a pot and wooden spoon or a box drum; a sheet of card raised as the cue. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | First in grade 4 because it reopens the dance space, and unit 6's challenges are solved by changing elements, which needs this unit's balance, rhythm and energy; the energy-words model starts here and unit 7's responses use it. Six ids, all performed except two to explain, so a showing day and a written day | Candidate: the invented dancer card read by the health course's lens for its food line | Food: no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body; food choices and eating are studied through the invented dancer only (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Bomba: its roots among enslaved Africans on Puerto Rico's sugar plantations are said in one plain sentence from a Puerto Rican cultural body's or a museum's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; no skirt work or costume; students make their own call-and-answer moves. Legong: Bali also has sacred dances that are never danced for show; legong is a dance made for watching, studied by watching, no costume or headdress, and eye moves are tried only as "focus", not copied as a style (leave-out line). Hazards and controls: as unit 1; small jumps on the student's own spot, landing softly through the feet and knees; no plank held more than 20 seconds; the drum played at a moderate volume. Connection: physical education grades 3 to 5 unit 15 (g4-dance-sequence) teaches dance sequences as motor skill in grade 4 weeks 27 to 30; this unit teaches no motor cue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 6 | 4 | Dances from facts and places. *How can facts from a school subject become a dance, and how does a dance style carry the place and time it comes from?* | 16 | da-g4-ideas; da-g4-connect | DA:Cr1.1.4a; DA:Cr1.1.4b; DA:Cn10.1.4a; DA:Cn10.1.4b; DA:Cn11.1.4a | Creating (Cr1); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: the gumboot dance (South Africa, made by Black mine workers in the gold mines around Johannesburg in the twentieth century, danced today), boots slapped and stamped in patterns, a piece by a named living group the unit plan picks, described and linked to a South African cultural body's or a museum's page; Liz Lerman, Ferocious Beauty: Genome (United States, 2006; Lerman born 1947, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a dance she made by working with scientists, described and linked to the artist's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 2's own starting points and challenge (da-g3-ideas) and research and culture ties (da-g3-connect). DA:Cr1.1.4a: name choreography ideas from music, pictures and experiences on an idea card. DA:Cr1.1.4b: invent a movement challenge for a partner, solved by changing dance elements. DA:Cn10.1.4a: link a watched dance's main idea to the student's own experience, or an invented child's, marked the same, and say how they match or differ. DA:Cn10.1.4b: research a school topic in two or three sources (from science grade 4's units taught by then: speed and collisions, energy on the move, or waves), choreograph its key facts as a group, and reflect on what dancing them showed. DA:Cn11.1.4a: describe the gumboot dance's moves and explain how they reflect the mines, the workers and their time. Words: choreography, inspiration, challenge, source, fact, era. Showing: each group's topic dance with its source list. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr1.1.4a, a kept card: the idea card with three ideas, each from a different kind of inspiration; DA:Cr1.1.4b, a look-for and a kept card: the challenge the student invented and the partner's solution shown, the element changed named on the card; DA:Cn10.1.4b, a look-for and kept notes: two or three key facts danced, each source named on the research notes, one reflection line; DA:Cn10.1.4a, a written item: an unseen described dance's main idea linked to an experience (the student's own or the invented child's), with how they are alike or different; DA:Cn11.1.4a, a written item: two moves of an unseen style on a new work card and how they reflect its place, era or community. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: several sources into one body of material and learning compared across subjects (grade 5). Tools: the idea card, challenge card, research notes sheet, source cards Curio Class writes from the science course's own sourced readings, work cards, Curio Class's own. Free path: pictures and music the class already has. Once-a-week path: 8 sessions | After unit 5 because an invented challenge is solved by changing the elements and rhythms just taught; before unit 7 because the study around one main idea starts from an idea card. The research-notes model starts here, and the topics come from science units taught by these weeks | Candidate: the source cards checked against the science course's readings, fact by fact | Gumboot dance: made by migrant mine workers who lived far from home under hard and unfair rules, said in one plain sentence from a South African cultural body's or a museum's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; no boots, no imitation of a people; students slap their own thighs gently and stamp softly in bare feet. Lerman: the work is studied only for how she worked with scientists; genes are not taught. Ties to the student's own life invited, never required, with the invented option; health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 7 | 4 | Styles inside one genre. *How do patterns shape a style or a theme across dances, and what makes a dance artistic?* | 16 | da-g4-form; da-g4-respond | DA:Cr2.1.4a; DA:Cr2.1.4b; DA:Re7.1.4a; DA:Re7.1.4b; DA:Re8.1.4a; DA:Re9.1.4a | Creating (Cr2); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9); connecting practiced through each style's setting (review). Works: locking (Los Angeles, about 1970, begun by Don Campbell, who died 2020) and popping (Fresno, California, 1970s, from the Electric Boogaloos), two styles inside hip-hop dance, each a piece by a named dancer the unit plan picks, described and linked to the dancer's or a museum's page; Merce Cunningham, Beach Birds (United States, 1991; Cunningham died 2009), a dance of birds at the shore whose order he set partly by chance, described and linked to the Cunningham trust's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 3's tools and forms (da-g3-form) and two traditions compared (da-g3-respond). DA:Cr2.1.4a: alter choreographic tools (repeat, reverse, add a level, change the order by a chance draw, as Cunningham did) to widen pattern and form choices, and discuss the choices. DA:Cr2.1.4b: build a dance study around one main idea and discuss how well the choices worked. DA:Re7.1.4a: spot the movement patterns across dances that shape a theme or a style. DA:Re7.1.4b: show and tell how locking and popping differ inside one genre, the showing done in the class's own phrase danced two ways from step cards written from named dancers' published teaching. DA:Re8.1.4a: decode a dance's meaning from its moves, its ideas and its setting together, in basic terms. DA:Re9.1.4a: discuss the traits that make a dance artistic, then apply them to dances watched. Class talk practices ELA's SL.4.1 (not owned). Words: theme, style, genre, setting, study, artistic. Showing: each group's dance study for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr2.1.4a, a look-for and a kept card: a tool altered on purpose in the study, named on the tools card with what it opened up; DA:Cr2.1.4b, a look-for and a kept line: the study's main idea is clear, and the line says how one choice worked; DA:Re7.1.4a, a written item: the pattern that carries one theme across two unseen phrases the teacher dances; DA:Re8.1.4a, a written item: the meaning of an unseen described dance from its moves, idea and setting on its work card; DA:Re9.1.4a, a written item: two traits of an artistic dance applied to the unseen dance ("It is artistic because ___, shown when ___"); DA:Re7.1.4b, a show item: the class phrase shown as locking and as popping, with one difference told. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: several tools altered together (grade 5). Tools: the tools card, chance cards (numbered cards drawn to set an order), the reason card and the response card, Curio Class's own. Free path: numbered scraps of paper drawn from a cup. Once-a-week path: 7 sessions | After unit 6 because the study grows from the idea card, and locking and popping are described with unit 5's energy words; before unit 8 because the study is reworked and staged there. The dance-reason-because model starts here | Candidate: a third style inside one genre, from outside the United States, as an extension card | Hip-hop: locking and popping were begun by Black and Latino young people in California; the dancers are named as their own pages name them; no costumes; the moves are tried as the class's own phrase after the dancers' published teaching. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; no floor spins, flips or other inverted moves; popping's muscle hits are small and never held. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 8 | 4 | Making a room a stage. *How do dancers rework a dance from comments, and how can a gym or a lawn feel like a real stage?* | 12 | da-g4-revise; da-g4-stage | DA:Cr3.1.4a; DA:Cr3.1.4b; DA:Pr6.1.4a; DA:Pr6.1.4b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr6); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: George Balanchine, The Firebird (United States, 1949, New York City Ballet), danced by Maria Tallchief (Osage Nation, 1925 to 2013), with costumes and scenery by Marc Chagall, described and linked to the company's or a museum's page; Urban Bush Women (United States, founded by Jawole Willa Jo Zollar in 1984, a company that dances today), dances made with and for communities in parks, streets and community halls, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 4's feedback and floor map (da-g3-revise) and stage areas and design choice (da-g3-stage). DA:Cr3.1.4a: rework the unit 7 study using classmates' comments and the student's own reflection to sharpen its intent, and explain the choices. DA:Cr3.1.4b: sketch how dancers stand in relation to each other (beside, in front, behind, above on a higher level). DA:Pr6.1.4a: plan how to make the gym, a hall or a lawn feel like a formal stage (where the audience sits, the entrances, a marked edge, a backdrop), paced out in steps, no measuring. DA:Pr6.1.4b: try different production elements (costume, music, scenery, a prop, light) and keep the one that best strengthens the dance's purpose for the audience. Words: rework, reflection, intent, relationship, formal stage, production. Showing: the year's dance for families in the planned space, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (one showing day and kept cards), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.4a, a kept card and a look-for: the rework card (two comments, the student's own reflection, the change and why), and the change seen in the showing; DA:Cr3.1.4b, a kept sketch: three dancers' relationships drawn and labeled for one moment of the dance; DA:Pr6.1.4a, a kept plan and a look-for: the stage plan, and the showing set up as planned; DA:Pr6.1.4b, a look-for and a kept line: the production element kept is used, and the program card says how it serves the dance's purpose. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: one dance fitted to two venues (grade 5), a change log (grade 5). Tools: the rework card, relationship sketch sheet, stage plan sheet and program card, Curio Class's own. Free path: a painted paper backdrop, chairs for the audience edge, a song or a drum. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in grade 4 because the dance reworked and staged is unit 7's study; the year-end check sits before it (week 26), outside the core checks of weeks 27 and 30 to 32, and this unit's family showing in weeks 27 to 32 is its own assessment | Candidate: a seated audience plan for a family member who uses a wheelchair, in every stage plan | Tallchief: of the Osage Nation, named as her own published words name her; the sensitivity agent reads the company's or a museum's page and an Osage Nation source first; no costume or imitation of the Firebird or of a people. Outdoors: the lawn checked for holes, sticks and glass; shade and water; the class stays in sight; bare feet only on checked grass. Light: no flashing, never pointed at eyes. Family yes for showing outside class; no photographs without it. Hazards and controls: as unit 1. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 9 | 5 | Rhythm from the body and the world. *How do dancers make rhythm with their bodies and the sounds around them, and how does an ensemble keep together and safe?* | 17 | da-g5-technique; da-g5-elements | DA:Pr5.1.5a; DA:Pr5.1.5b; DA:Pr5.1.5c; DA:Pr4.1.5a; DA:Pr4.1.5b; DA:Pr4.1.5c | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding touched on the works (review); creating touched by the ensemble's body-percussion phrase. Works: sasa (Samoa, a group dance danced today, often seated, with fast, tight slaps, claps and arm moves in unison to a drum), a piece by a named group the unit plan picks, described and linked to a Samoan cultural body's or a museum's page; Doris Humphrey, Water Study (United States, 1928; Humphrey died 1958), flowing waves of movement begun in the torso, danced to no music but the dancers' breath, described and linked to an archive's page; Stomp (United Kingdom, first staged in Brighton in 1991 by Luke Cresswell and Steve McNicholas, performed today), rhythm made with brooms, tubs and everyday things, described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 5's balance, cues and strengthening (da-g4-technique) and drum parts, eye contact and energy words (da-g4-elements). DA:Pr5.1.5a: recall and perform a run of phrases with alignment and clarity. DA:Pr5.1.5b: practice safe body habits (warm-up, soft landings, rest, water) and discuss how they and nutrition help prevent injury, through an invented dancer. DA:Pr5.1.5c: rehearse with the ensemble to synchronize timing and refine spacing, and set goals from feedback (the goal card). DA:Pr4.1.5a: combine shapes with ground paths and air paths (small jumps and leaps), connect through eye focus and project outward to an audience. DA:Pr4.1.5b: dance to rhythms from inside the body (claps, slaps, stamps, breath) and from outside it (found sounds), and respond when the timing changes. DA:Pr4.1.5c: contrast tight and flowing movement started from the torso or the limbs, and analyze the energy. Words: ensemble, synchronize, project, body percussion, tight, flowing. Showing: the ensemble's body-percussion dance for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Pr5.1.5a, a look-for: a run of three phrases recalled with alignment and clarity; DA:Pr5.1.5c, a look-for and a kept card: unison timing and even spacing, and the goal card's goal set from feedback; DA:Pr4.1.5a, a look-for: ground and air paths with eye focus and projection; DA:Pr4.1.5b, a look-for: the group dances to its own body percussion and a found-sound rhythm and keeps on when the teacher changes the timing; DA:Pr5.1.5b, a written item: from a new invented dancer's card, two safe habits and how they and food help prevent injury; DA:Pr4.1.5c, a show item and a written item: one move shown tight and then flowing at the teacher's spot, saying where it starts, and one line analyzing the energy of the teacher's unseen phrase. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: partner and group sections in measures of three and four (grade 6). Tools: the run card, goal card, body-percussion card and invented dancer card, Curio Class's own; a beat keeper or a hand drum. Free path: plastic tubs, paper and the body for found sound. Once-a-week path: 9 sessions | First in grade 5 because it reopens the dance space, and the ensemble habits and goal card carry every later rehearsal of the year; 17 days because a run of phrases rehearsed to goals needs a second rehearsal cycle after the critique. The rehearsal-goals model starts here | Candidate: the found-sound kit checked by the safety agent item by item | Food: no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body; nutrition is studied through the invented dancer only (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Sasa: the sensitivity agent reads a Samoan cultural body's or a museum's page first; no costume; slaps gentle, on the student's own thighs and the floor. Found sound: plastic tubs, paper and soft sticks only; no metal lids, bins or glass; a moderate volume and a rest after loud passages. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; air paths are small jumps and low leaps on a clear path, landing softly; no lifts. Connection: physical education grades 3 to 5 unit 22 (g5-gym-dance-sequence) teaches a routine of dance parts as motor skill in grade 5 weeks 22 to 25; this unit teaches no motor cue. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 10 | 5 | Many sources, opposing themes. *How do dancers build material from many sources, and how can dances of the same years carry opposite feelings?* | 17 | da-g5-ideas; da-g5-connect | DA:Cr1.1.5a; DA:Cr1.1.5b; DA:Cn10.1.5a; DA:Cn10.1.5b; DA:Cn11.1.5a | Creating (Cr1); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); responding practiced on the works (review). Works: Lin Hwai-min, Cursive (Taiwan, 2001, Cloud Gate Dance Theatre of Taiwan, founded 1973; Lin born 1947, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), a dance built from brush calligraphy and slow, flowing training, described and linked to the company's page; Pearl Primus, Hard Time Blues (United States, 1943; Primus, born in Trinidad, 1919 to 1994), a solo about the hard lives of Black sharecroppers in the South, with huge leaps, described and linked to an archive's or a museum's page; the Lindy hop at Harlem's Savoy Ballroom in the 1930s (United States; Frankie Manning, 1914 to 2009, among its dancers), a joyful partner social dance, described and linked: two African American dances of the same years with opposing themes | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 6's idea card and invented challenge (da-g4-ideas) and research notes and a style's era (da-g4-connect). DA:Cr1.1.5a: build dance material from several inspirations (a story, nature, a news card Curio Class writes from a public agency's news release), each with its source line. DA:Cr1.1.5b: invent and solve several movement challenges in a small group to build material. DA:Cn10.1.5a: contrast two dances with opposing themes (Hard Time Blues and the Lindy hop), discussing the feelings they raise, with feeling words from a card, and each dance's viewpoint. DA:Cn10.1.5b: research a topic from another subject (science grade 5's particles of matter in solids, liquids and gases), dance it as a group, and compare how dancing it and the science lessons taught it. DA:Cn11.1.5a: describe how a style's movement qualities express its culture's or period's ideas (Cursive's