d times. Lindy hop: its aerials and throws are watched only; the class's partner moves are danced side by side, holding hands only if both agree, no lifts or throws. Cursive: Taiwan is named as the company names itself; calligraphy is tried with water on paper or in the air, no ink. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; leaps small, landing softly. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 11 | 5 | How dance speaks without words. *How do dancers shape a study so it speaks without words, and what makes a dance meaningful?* | 14 | da-g5-form; da-g5-respond | DA:Cr2.1.5a; DA:Cr2.1.5b; DA:Re7.1.5a; DA:Re7.1.5b; DA:Re8.1.5a; DA:Re9.1.5a | Creating (Cr2); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9); connecting practiced through the works' settings (review). Works: Crystal Pite, Emergence (Canada, 2009, for the National Ballet of Canada; Pite born 1970, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), dancers moving as a swarm of insects, patterns that carry the meaning, described and linked to the company's page; a staged piece by Les Ballets Africains the unit plan picks (Guinea, founded in Paris in 1952 by FodAcba KeA_ta, Guinea's national company from 1958, dancing today), a piece made for audiences, not a ceremony, described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 7's study and altered tool (da-g4-form) and styles in a genre, meaning from setting and artistic traits (da-g4-respond). DA:Cr2.1.5a: alter several choreographic tools together to open options and sharpen the central idea, and explain the choices. DA:Cr2.1.5b: build a study from moves chosen from unit 10's material, and discuss how dance says things without words. DA:Re7.1.5a: find a dance's meaning or purpose from its movement patterns (Emergence's swarm). DA:Re7.1.5b: describe a dance style the student chooses, their own culture's if they choose to share it, or one from the class's style cards (marked the same), in basic terms, and compare it with another style. DA:Re8.1.5a: interpret a dance from its movements, explaining how they convey its main idea. DA:Re9.1.5a: define the traits of an artistic, meaningful dance and tie each to a dance element. The written response practices ELA's W.5.1, an opinion with reasons (not owned). Words: central idea, without words, interpret, meaning, purpose, traits. Showing: each group's study for a partner class. End-of-unit evidence (a showing day and a written day), id by id: DA:Cr2.1.5a, a look-for and a kept card: two tools altered together in the study, named on the tools card with how they sharpen the idea; DA:Cr2.1.5b, a look-for and a kept line: the study is built from chosen moves, and the line says how it speaks without words; DA:Re7.1.5b, a kept card made on the last making day: the style card (the chosen style described in three dance terms and compared with one other); DA:Re7.1.5a, a written item: the purpose of the teacher's unseen phrase from its pattern; DA:Re8.1.5a, a written item: an interpretation of an unseen described dance with two moves that carry its main idea; DA:Re9.1.5a, a written item: an opinion naming two traits of a meaningful dance, each tied to an element, with reasons. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a written critique with the student's own criteria (grade 6). Tools: the tools card, style cards, the opinion frame and the response card, Curio Class's own. Free path: as unit 10. Once-a-week path: 6 sessions | After unit 10 because the study is chosen from its material; 14 days because tools and forms return from grades 3 and 4, so the study builds faster; before unit 12 because the study is the dance refined and staged there | Candidate: a style card set with at least six styles from five regions, so no student must use their own | No student is asked to name, show or explain their own family's culture: the style cards are offered beside it and marked the same. Les Ballets Africains: some Guinean dances come from ceremonies; the unit uses a piece the company presents for audiences, and the sensitivity agent reads the company's own pages and a Guinean cultural body's or a museum's page first; no costume or imitation of a people (leave-out line). Health's steps if a student tells something. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; the swarm study keeps an arm's length, no touching in the crowd. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |
| 12 | 5 | One dance, two spaces. *How do dancers refine a dance from peer suggestions, and how does a dance change to fit two different spaces?* | 12 | da-g5-revise; da-g5-stage | DA:Cr3.1.5a; DA:Cr3.1.5b; DA:Pr6.1.5a; DA:Pr6.1.5b | Creating (Cr3); performing (Pr6); responding practiced on the two works (review). Works: frevo (Recife, Brazil, begun in the late 1800s; on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage since 2012), danced in the streets at carnival and on stages, with small umbrellas, described and linked to UNESCO's or a Brazilian cultural body's page; Trisha Brown, Roof Piece (United States, 1971), dancers on rooftops across New York passing moves from roof to roof, described and linked to the company's page (Brown from unit 3, a new work in a new space) | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: unit 8's rework and relationship sketch (da-g4-revise) and stage plan and production elements (da-g4-stage). DA:Cr3.1.5a: try peer suggestions in movement to widen the options in the unit 11 study, and explain the refinements kept. DA:Cr3.1.5b: log what changed in the sequence in words, drawings or a recording (the change log). DA:Pr6.1.5a: adapt the dance to two venues (the classroom and the gym, a hall or a lawn) by changing its spacing and moves. DA:Pr6.1.5b: choose staging features that strengthen the dance's purpose and suit both venues. Words: suggestion, refine, log, venue, adapt, staging. Showing: the dance in two spaces, the second for families, with the family's yes for any showing outside class. End-of-unit evidence (two showing days and a kept log), id by id: DA:Cr3.1.5a, a look-for and a kept line: a peer suggestion tried and kept is seen in the showing, and the log's line explains the refinement; DA:Cr3.1.5b, a kept log: at least three changes logged in words or drawings, each dated; DA:Pr6.1.5a, a look-for across both showing days: spacing and moves changed to fit each venue; DA:Pr6.1.5b, a look-for and a kept note: the staging features chosen work in both venues, and the staging note says how they serve the purpose. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: performance manners and the choreographer's notes (grade 6). Tools: the suggestion card, change log, venue plan sheet and staging note, Curio Class's own. Free path: paper umbrellas, chairs for the audience edge, the class's voices for music. Once-a-week path: 5 sessions | Last in the band because the dance refined and staged is unit 11's study; the year-end check sits before it (week 26), outside the core checks of weeks 29 to 32; the two showings in weeks 27 to 32 are its own assessment, and the thread then goes to grade 6's production terms | Candidate: the second venue offered as a recorded showing for a school with one room only | Frevo: its carnival roots are said from UNESCO's and a Brazilian cultural body's page, read first by the sensitivity agent; no costume; umbrellas small, paper or soft plastic, with no points or metal ribs. Roof Piece: no rooftop, balcony, stage edge or height of any kind; both venues are at ground level. Recording: a change log may be a recording only with the family's yes, of feet or hands, a face never required. Family yes for showing outside class; no photographs without it. Hazards and controls: as unit 1; frevo's quick steps danced low and on the spot, no squats held. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving). |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Year 3 mid-year (week 17, days 33 and 34) | 2 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 1 and 2 in their own year, as a showing in groups of five: a copied sequence recalled with the body aligned (DA:Pr5.1.3a, DA:Pr5.1.3c), an improvisation that fills a new set count with two energy qualities (DA:Pr4.1.3b, DA:Pr4.1.3c), and a solution to a new challenge card (DA:Cr1.1.3b, unit 2). Unit 2's own end-of-unit records (days 31 and 32) are carried unscored; no challenge card from unit 2 repeats. Respond on day 34. |
| Year 3 year-end (week 26, days 51 and 52) | 3 | 1 | 1 | Samples units 1 to 3. Written part first: two moves of a new described dance linked to the culture on its work card (DA:Cn11.1.3a, unit 2) and one alike and one different between two new traditions' cards (DA:Re9.1.3a, unit 3). Then the showing: a partner level, direction and path change (DA:Pr5.1.3b, unit 1) and moves from a new starting point of the student's own (DA:Cr1.1.3a, unit 2). Unit 3's other records (days 49 and 50) are carried unscored. Unit 4 is assessed by its own family showing. Respond on day 52. |
