228:| 6 | K | Moving makes me strong. *What happens to my body when I move, and which activities make my heart and body strong?* | 8 | k-fitness-activities; k-body-notices | SHAPE.S2.2.6; SHAPE.S2.2.10 | Cues: "fast feet, hot face, quick breath"; the three effort faces; "freeze on the signal" (unit 1, review). Settings: rest, walk, run and dance circuits | Name activities that make the body strong and keep the heart healthy and sort them from sitting activities in a picture sort (S2.2.6); notice breath, warmth and heartbeat change while moving, and choose an effort face (easy, medium, hard) after rest, walking and running (S2.2.10; effort-scale introduced). Opens with a retrieval task on health K to 2 unit 2 (kindergarten weeks 6 to 11): playing hard makes the heart beat faster; PE adds the noticing and the effort faces while moving. Fitness is a word and a habit, never a score. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: travel and freeze on the signal in the circuit, unit 1's cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge prompts: name three activities that build fitness; choose a face after two kinds of movement and say one body change; conduct: stops on the signal, S3.2.4 review), 1 respond day. Tools: picture sort cards and effort faces (ours) | After the skill units because the circuits use the year's locomotor and ball skills (review); it sits late in the year so the body-change talk rests on months of moving; eight days because its two rows are knowledge rows taught inside circuits that move the whole time | Candidate: a home-activity picture card for the family note | Bodies: no weighing, no body talk; a child who prefers not to run shows effort by another movement; a child with asthma or a heart condition follows the nurse's guidance. Water break and shade on warm outdoor days. Sensitivity read sources: the national pediatrics association's pages on young children's activity, the federal physical activity guidelines' children's page. Cost: $0 |
229:| 7 | K | Moving and feeling. *How can moving show a feeling, and how does moving help me feel well?* | 10 | k-express-feel | SHAPE.S4.2.1; SHAPE.S4.2.3 | Cues: "show it with your whole body"; "bend, swing, land soft" (unit 1, review). Settings: animal walks, a scarf dance to a slow and a fast song, review stations of the year's skills | Name movement activities where a child can show feelings (animal walks, a scarf dance) (S4.2.1) and name ways moving helps them feel well: more energy, calmer after a slow dance, a heart made strong by moving (unit 6, review) (S4.2.3, in full: ways, not one feeling). Review stations of the year's skills (units 1 to 5) close each day, where the child says how a move felt, so grade 1 can retrieve every kindergarten cue; the choosing of unit 5 returns (review). Ends: 1 check day (skill task: soft landing in an animal jump, unit 1's cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge prompts: one activity and one feeling; two ways moving helps me feel well; conduct: shares space with words in the scarf dance, S3.2.3 review), 1 respond day | Last in kindergarten: it is the year's expressive movement and dance unit (dance returns in units 13 and 21), it builds S4.2.3 on unit 6's body changes, and its review stations hand every cue to grade 1. It follows the year-end check (week 28) because kindergarten weeks 29 to 32 hold the core subjects' year-end checks (CALENDAR.md); its own end-of-unit assessment closes the year | Candidate: a class "how moving makes us feel" picture wall with no names | Emotions: no right answers; a child may step out for a reset with a role; the teacher page names the school counselor. Music: a scarf dance with no cultural claim. Cost: $0 (reuses unit 3's scarves) |
235:| 13 | 1 | Dance to the beat. *How do dances from different places use the beat, and how can my body show fast, slow, high and low?* | 12 | g1-dance-moves; g1-dance-ideas | SHAPE.S1.2.15; SHAPE.S2.2.5 | Cues: "listen to the beat, follow the leader". Settings: a circle dance, a line dance, a scarf or ribbon dance | Traveling, in-place and object-handling moves taken from a few dance styles: step to the beat, turn, clap, slide, and a scarf or ribbon moved to the beat (S1.2.15); move to a fast and to a slow beat, show high and low shapes, change direction on a signal (S2.2.5). Picks: Seven Jumps, a circle dance danced in Denmark (Syvspring) and the Netherlands (De Zevensprong), named "a folk dance from Denmark and the Netherlands" because its origin is claimed by more than one country; La Raspa, a traditional Mexican children's dance; and a scarf dance with no cultural claim. Opens with a retrieval task on kindergarten's in-place moves and traveling. Ends: 2 check days (skill task: moves to the beat in a simple pattern; knowledge task shown by moving: changes speed when the music changes; conduct: kind to a partner in the circle, S3.2.5 review), 1 respond day. Tools: music track or the teacher's drum; Curio Class writes the dance cards and each dance's short source line | Dance follows a year of traveling and in-place control; it sits before the year-end check so the check samples it | Candidate: one dance a family shares, used only where its source says it is open to be taught | Cultural content: each dance named by its place, taught with respect; no costume or imitation of a people; sacred or ceremonial dances never used (Cory, 2026-11-20). La Raspa is often taught in a double circle split by gender: here partners are set by the teacher, with no gender roles. Communities: Danish, Dutch and Mexican folk dance traditions. Spot-checked 2026-11-25: Seven Jumps at a folk dance notes archive (folkdancefootnotes.org, the Syvspring and Zevensprong entry), La Raspa at a folk dance teaching sheet; the agent studies a national folklife archive's entries and each country's own cultural institution's page first. Swap allowed (Swaps). Cost: $0 |
236:| 14 | 1 | Games from many places. *What games do children play in other places, and which do I like to play alone or with others?* | 10 | g1-world-games; g1-together-favorites; g1-do-well | SHAPE.S3.2.10; SHAPE.S4.2.2; SHAPE.S4.2.4; SHAPE.S4.2.7 | Cues: each game's one rule card; "eyes up, light feet, change on the signal" (unit 8, review); "star and wish". Settings: a freeze-and-go game, a circle game with a rotating chaser and no one out, a favorite chosen | Name and play movement games from different cultures, each named by where people play it, shown on a world map (S3.2.10). Picks: Mugunghwa kkochi pieotseumnida ("the rose of Sharon has bloomed"), a freeze-and-go alleyway game from Korea, played with no one out (a child seen moving goes back three steps); and Doña Blanca, a circle game from Mexico (a different game from unit 13's dance), its chaser rotating so no one is out. Point out activities that let a child be with others (S4.2.2, at naming level: a favorite and whether it is alone or with others) and pick favorites (S4.2.4); notice what the child does well and where more practice would help, a star and a wish after a skill day (S4.2.7; feeds the goal cycle). Uses calm steps from unit 11 (review). Opens with a retrieval task on kindergarten unit 5's choosing a station and unit 7's how moving feels. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: travel and freeze with a change of speed in the freeze-and-go game, unit 8's cue watched at the unit's first skill day and again here; knowledge prompts: one game and one place; a favorite and alone or with others; a star and a wish on a picture card; conduct: plays the no-one-out rule kindly, S3.2.5 review), 1 respond day | Last in grade 1 because favorites and a star and a wish look back on the whole year; it follows the year-end check (week 28) because grade 1 weeks 29 to 32 hold the core year-end checks, and social studies grade 1 has taught maps by then (Cross-subject). Its game picks avoid SEL K to 2's games from other places (Ampe from Ghana in its unit 5, jan-ken from Japan in its unit 11) | Candidate: a class book of the children's own family games, only with family consent | Cultural content: communities named are Korean and Mexican. Mugunghwa spot-checked 2026-11-25: Korea's Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism page "Korea's Culture of Play" (from Korea.net, https://www.mcst.go.kr/english/useful/month202112.jsp) shows it among Korea's alleyway games; it is introduced only as a children's game played in Korea, with no reference to any television show. The agent studies a Korean cultural or education institution's page and a national folklife archive's entry on Doña Blanca first. No game that is sacred or ceremonial. Swap allowed. Cost: $0 |
241:| 19 | 2 | Long-handled striking. *How do I hit a ball off a tee with a long bat or stick, and why do rules keep us safe and fair?* | 9 | g2-long-strike; g2-rules-fair | SHAPE.S1.2.14; SHAPE.S3.2.6; SHAPE.S3.2.9 | Cues: "side to the target, eyes on the ball, level swing". Settings: a tee or a self-drop, in place, one swinger per space | Hit a ball from a tee or a self-drop with a long-handled tool (plastic bat, hockey-style stick) in several in-place tasks (S1.2.14); explain how following rules keeps everyone safe and treats people fairly (S3.2.6) and, with teacher guidance, make fair choices about who goes first and how to share (S3.2.9). Opens with a retrieval task on SEL K to 2 unit 13 (grade 2 weeks 1 to 4), where SEL teaches who a rule helps; PE teaches why striking rules keep a swinger and a watcher safe. Team talk from unit 17 returns (review). Opens also with a retrieval task on grade 1 unit 12's short-tool striking. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: tee contact with a level swing; knowledge prompt: says why a striking rule helps; conduct: follows the rule and makes a fair choice in the moment, who goes first at the tee or how to share the bat, watched by the teacher), 1 respond day | Long tools are first expected in grade 2 (from the tool) and follow short-tool control; the swing-space rules make this the unit where rules and fairness have a real reason | Candidate: the class writes its own three striking-station rules with pictures | Full swing space cleared; one swinger per space; stand beside, never in front; foam balls. Safety read (striking tools). Cost: $60 (8 plastic bats and balls; pool noodles $10 on the low-equipment path) |
243:| 21 | 2 | Dance sequences. *How can I link three moves into a dance with a start and an end, and show its level, path and speed?* | 7 | g2-dance-sequence; g2-show-ideas | SHAPE.S2.2.4 | Cues: "start shape, three moves, end shape". Settings: one dance style, then another, with a partner; a scarf or ribbon in the sequence | Link three dance moves into a short sequence with a start and an end shape, in one style and then another: used in a new setting, SHAPE.S1.2.15 (owned in grade 1, unit 13). Perform a movement and name its idea in traveling, in place and with an object: move at a low level, travel a curvy path, and a scarf or ribbon moved along a path or at a level in the dance, each named in one sentence (S2.2.4, in full). Picks: Kinderpolka, a German children's partner dance, and Paw Paw Patch, a US children's play-party game sung and danced in lines, neither used in grade 1. Opens with a retrieval task on grade 1 unit 13's dance moves and unit 8's movement words. Ends: 1 check day (skill task: a three-move sequence to the beat; knowledge task shown while moving and said in one sentence, one movement and one scarf movement; conduct: encourages a partner, S3.2.2 review), 1 respond day | After unit 20, so the class has named pathways under pressure; dance returns in every year of the band (units 7, 13, 21), so it is never taught once and dropped | Candidate: partners teach each other their sequence | Cultural content: German and US folk traditions; partners set by the teacher with no gender roles; Paw Paw Patch's verse uses an invented name, never a child singled out. Spot-checked 2026-11-25: Kinderpolka at a folk tune archive and a folk dance teaching sheet; Paw Paw Patch listed as a US children's play-party game at a folk dance notes archive (folkdancefootnotes.org); the agent studies a national folklife archive's entries first. Swap allowed. Cost: $0 (reuses scarves) |
